1. **Install Unity Hub and Unity Editor:**
   - Download and install Unity Hub from the official Unity website: https://unity.com/download
   - Unity Hub is a separate application that manages your Unity Editor installations and projects.
   - During installation, make sure to select the "3D" template, as you're interested in creating a 3D game.

2. **Create a new 3D project:**
   - Open Unity Hub and click on the "New" button.
   - Select "3D" template and give your project a name (e.g., "MyFirst3DGame").
   - Choose a location to save your project and click "Create."

3. **Familiarize yourself with the Unity Editor:**
   - Open your newly created project in Unity Editor.
   - The Unity Editor window is divided into several parts:
     - **Scene:** Where you design and preview your game.
     - **Game:** Displays the game view with camera perspective.
     - **Hierarchy:** Lists all the objects in your scene.
     - **Inspector:** Displays properties and components of the selected object.
     - **Project:** Lists all the assets (scripts, models, textures, etc.) in your project.
     - **Console:** Displays debug messages and errors.

4. **Create a new C# script:**
   - In the Project window, right-click and select "Create > C# Script."
   - Name the script (e.g., "PlayerMovement") and double-click to open it in your preferred code editor (e.g., Visual Studio, Visual Studio Code, or MonoDevelop).

5. **Write the PlayerMovement script:**
   - Here's a simple script to move a box and make it jump using Unity's built-in physics engine:

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class PlayerMovement : MonoBehaviour
{
    public float moveSpeed = 5.0f;
    public float jumpForce = 7.0f;
    private Rigidbody rb;
    private bool isGrounded;

    void Start()
    {
        rb = GetComponent<Rigidbody>();
    }

    void Update()
    {
        float moveX = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");
        float moveZ = Input.GetAxis("Vertical");

        Vector3 movement = new Vector3(moveX, 0, moveZ) * moveSpeed * Time.deltaTime;
        transform.Translate(movement);

        if (Input.GetButtonDown("Jump") && isGrounded)
        {
            rb.AddForce(Vector3.up * jumpForce, ForceMode.Impulse);
            isGrounded = false;
        }
    }

    void OnCollisionEnter(Collision collision)
    {
        if (collision.gameObject.CompareTag("Ground"))
        {
            isGrounded = true;
        }
    }
}
```

6. **Attach the script to the box:**
   - In the Hierarchy window, find the "Main Camera" object and delete it, as you'll create your own camera later.
   - Right-click in the Hierarchy window and select "3D Object > Cube" to create a new box.
   - Select the box, and in the Inspector window, click "Add Component > Physics > Rigidbody" to add a Rigidbody component.
   - Drag and drop the "PlayerMovement" script onto the box in the Hierarchy window to attach it.

7. **Create a simple plane for the ground:**
   - Right-click in the Hierarchy window and select "3D Object > Plane" to create a new plane.
   - Rename the plane to "Ground" in the Inspector window.
   - Add a "Tag" component to the plane and set its value to "Ground."

8. **Create a new camera:**
   - Right-click in the Hierarchy window and select "Camera" to create a new camera.
   - Position the camera behind the box to create a third-person perspective.

9. **Test your game:**
   - Click the "Play" button in the Unity Editor toolbar to test your game.
   - Use the arrow keys or WASD to move the box, and the spacebar to make it jump.

Congratulations! You've created a simple 3D game with a moving box and jumping functionality using Unity. This is a great starting point, and you can now build upon this foundation to create more complex games. Good luck, and have fun coding!
